  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Replace the Human Rights Act with a British Bill of Rights; update communications powers and new tools against non-violent extremism; maintain strong Armed Forces and renew the nuclear deterrent; target net migration in the tens of thousands with stricter deportation and language requirements; eliminate the deficit and run budget surpluses; welfare cap, benefit freeze, lower household cap, and stricter conditionality; publish gender pay gap data and aim to halve the disability employment gap; increase NHS spending by at least £8bn with seven-day services and expanded mental health access. TEXT_D: Leave the EU and end freedom of movement with a points-based system; five-year moratorium on unskilled migration and limits on skilled visas; increase Border Agency staff and deport foreign offenders; scrap the bedroom tax and keep Housing Benefit for under-25s; national referenda via citizen initiatives, robust recall, proportional voting; spend at least 2% of GDP on defence and retain the nuclear deterrent.",
  "reasoning": "The profile puts highest weight on Authority and Proportionality (with Loyalty next), and TEXT_E emphasizes institutional authority (British Bill of Rights, policing and surveillance powers), fiscal discipline and welfare conditionality, plus strong defence, while still addressing Care and moderate Equality. TEXT_D is strong on Loyalty and border control but weakens deference to established authority through sweeping citizen-initiated referenda and reverses some welfare stringency, so overall it aligns less with the weighted priorities.",
